# cap_test manifest — the first policy this system has ever shipped (#432).
#
# 🔴 IT IS HERE TO BE REFUSED SOMETHING.  cap_test legitimately asks
# cap_server for block-device capabilities: arms [3] and [5] open a
# partition with one.  It has no business driving PCI hardware -- it is a
# test, not a driver -- and #432's whole argument is that the right to map
# memory for a device must come from somewhere other than holding the
# master device port.  This file is that somewhere, and the arm that asks
# for a PCI capability must come back denied.
#
# ⚠️ AND IT IS THE FIRST ON x86-64, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME AS THE FIRST.
# i386 has compiled and shipped manifests for a while -- cpustat, ush,
# hello_exec, dlopen_test, virtual_terminal_server -- and cap_server
# provisions from them on every i386 boot.  x86-64 shipped none, because
# mkmanifest was built only for the target whose userland is complete: the
# mechanism was absent from this target rather than from the system.
#
# 🔥 The first version of this comment said the machinery had "never once
# run", which was false and was reached by a search that could not have
# found the answer -- `find -name "*.cmf" -not -path "*/build*"', looking
# for compiled output everywhere except where compiled output goes.

name cap_test

# RESOURCE_BLK_DEVICE (4), any device, CAP_OP_BLK_READ|WRITE (0x3).
#
# ⚠️ `any' and not a device id, and the difference is now sayable: v2 gave
# an entry a resource_id, so "block devices" and "THIS block device" stopped
# being the same sentence.  cap_test opens whichever of ahci0a and
# virtio_blk0a the board happens to have, so `any' is what it means.
required 4 any 0x3

# ── And deliberately NOT this ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# required 5 0x010601 0x7    RESOURCE_PCI_DEVICE, SATA AHCI, DMA|MMIO|IRQ
#
# The line above is what block_device_server would declare.  Its absence
# here is the policy, and cap_test [13] is what reads it back.
